From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: emuTOS-MCD <darekadumon@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:56:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 088/184] segacd: the CDBIOS lives -- exchanged back in,
 called, exchanged out

Phase one of the timeshare. The loader photographs the CDBIOS into
Word RAM at 0xBA000 on its way past eviction -- the machine's own
firmware, still inside the machine it shipped in, parked above the end
of EmuTOS's code where the Atari memory map has no opinion about it.
When a sector is wanted, low memory and the park exchange contents,
Sega's code runs, and they exchange back.

Two crashes on the way, both instructive, both now in the comments:

Interrupts must stay masked across the WHOLE visit, not each exchange.
Between them the vector table at 0 is Sega's while every variable
EmuTOS's handlers would touch is parked, so one tick in that window
runs one OS's handler against the other's state.

And the visit needs its own stack. EmuTOS's supervisor stack lives at
0x800, inside the 24 KB being exchanged, so the first swap turned the
exchanging code's own return address into BIOS bytes. You cannot
exchange the ground you are standing on; the scratch stack sits in
Word RAM between EmuTOS's code and the parked firmware.

Result in emulation: CDBIOS probe 0000 swaps 2 -- CDBCHK executed and
answered 'drive not busy' -- with EmuTOS surviving the round trip and
the diagnostic running on through TEST 8 afterwards.
